<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-models" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Modeling</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      A lot of what mathematics aims to do is to describe situations with formulas and rules. That is essentially what modeling is.
    </p>
    <p>
      Very rarely will our model (the mathematical formula we determine) match the real world situation perfectly.
      So, we should aim to remove the perfection goal from our brains, and focus on the best model for the situation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Which model is the best very much depends on the situation as well of the goals of the model.
      What do we want to prioritize?
      Would we rather have a more complicated mathematical formula that is difficult to wrap our brains around if it means better accuracy?
      Or, would we sacrifice a bit of accuracy in favor of something that is easier to use and understand?
      And, what does <q>use the model</q> mean? Are we using it to predict future values of the quantity we're modeling?
      Then, we might give up a little bit of accuracy of the model for data that happened long ago if it fits the more recent data better.
    </p>
    <p>
      Successful modeling is more about understanding the premise and the goals, than any mathematical fanciness.
    </p>
    <p>
      In this chapter we'll focus on two types of models, linear and exponential; the differences between them; and how to to use each of them.
    </p>
  </introduction>

